<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n4266" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Ătīnĭus</span></b> , a,
			um, adj.
		<div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>Name of a Roman</i> gens, e. g. C. Atinius Labeo,
			etc.— </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			Derivv. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>A.</b> Atinia lex,
				<i><span class="en">Cic.
				Verr. 2, 1, 42</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Gell. 17, 7</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Dig. 41, 3,
				4</span></i>; cf. Hugo, Rechtsgesch. p.
			381.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>B.</b> Atinia
			ulmus, <i>a kind of elm-tree</i>, <i>the
				loose-flowering elm</i>: Ulmus effusa, Willd.; <i><span class="en">Col. 5, 6,
			2</span></i> and 9; <i><span class="en">id. Arb. 16, 1</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Plin. 16,
				17, 29, § 72</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
</div>